# Hypothesis (data-grounded, association only)

**Question:** How does the effect of increasing education expenditure on per-capita GDP compare between
developing countries and countries in Sub-Saharan Africa?

## Hypothesis

> In this panel, raising education expenditure is **not** associated with higher per-capita income in either
> group — and where a slope can be measured at all it is **negative in both**, but **about 6.6x steeper in raw
> units for the Lower-middle-income (LMC) "developing" group (−0.66 log points per +1pp of GNI, 95% CI
> [−1.01, −0.31], n=5) than for Sub-Saharan Africa (−0.10, 95% CI [−0.15, −0.05], n=5)**. That group gap is,
> however, **an artefact of scale, not of sensitivity**: in variance-standardised terms the two groups are
> statistically indistinguishable (within-window r = −0.96 LMC vs −0.97 SSF; standardised group difference
> +0.008, 95% CI [−0.53, +0.54], p=0.97, n=10), because SSA's education-expenditure share moved 2.9x more
> than LMC's in the only comparable window (SD 0.223pp vs 0.076pp). Over the 41-year series the
> education→income link is **fragile and specification-driven** (LMC growth on lagged expenditure: −0.023,
> 95% CI [−0.064, +0.018], p=0.27, n=40 without exports; −0.028, p=0.18 with exports; +0.002, p=0.91 in a
> dynamic AR(1) spec; only the enrollment-controlled spec is "significant" at −0.048, p=0.010, which
> over-controls the total effect). By contrast the **observable channel that does carry a real group
> difference is education investment → enrolment, not → income**: SSA expanded primary gross enrolment by
> +36.4pp and secondary by +28.6pp over 1975–2015 while its education-expenditure share **fell** 1.15pp,
> whereas LMC raised its share by 0.37pp for +22.7pp / +36.7pp of enrolment; in short-run differenced terms a
> +1pp rise in the expenditure share predicts **no** enrolment gain in either group (LMC −0.78pp primary, 95%
> CI [−2.28, +0.71], p=0.30, n=40, and −0.79pp secondary, 95% CI [−1.71, +0.14], p=0.09; SSF +0.25pp primary,
> 95% CI [−1.27, +1.77], p=0.74, and −0.26pp secondary, 95% CI [−0.91, +0.38], p=0.41; group contrasts
> p=0.34 and p=0.52, n=80). Hence the honest comparative statement is: **Sub-Saharan Africa spends a larger
> share of national income on education (mean 4.30% vs 3.50% of GNI) and converted it into larger primary
> enrolment gains, yet shows a flatter — not steeper — raw per-capita-income association than the
> lower-middle-income comparison group, and no specification in these data supports a positive
> education-expenditure → per-capita-income effect for either group.**
>
> Standing caveats built into the claim: "per-capita GDP" is measured by **GNI per capita (constant 2015
> US$)** because no GDP-per-capita series exists in the file; "developing countries" = the **Lower middle
> income (LMC)** group, the only other aggregate present; education expenditure = **Adjusted savings:
> education expenditure (% of GNI)** (the "% of total government expenditure" series named in the dataset
> description is absent); both are **group aggregates, not countries**, so within-group cross-country
> variation is unavailable and N=2 units; and education expenditure is denominated in GNI, so a fall in
> income mechanically inflates the ratio — which, together with the fact that in 2011–2015 incomes rose while
> the education share fell in both groups, is the most likely source of the negative co-movement.
> **Association, not causation.**

## Why the 2011–2015 window is the only direct answer (and why it is weak)

`GNI per capita` exists for **all 41 years for LMC but only 5 years (2011–2015) for SSA**. No other window
contains income for both groups. The direct comparison therefore rests on **n=5 vs n=5**, on points that are
near-monotone in-window, so naive intervals understate uncertainty (a Bartlett effective-sample-size
correction is reported alongside: `r=-0.960 [-0.999, +0.016]` LMC and `r=-0.967 [-0.999, -0.090]` SSF).
Nothing was imputed and no external data was used, so the 36 missing SSA income years remain a gap.

